[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:A Chance in a Million]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.13]Lesson 36 | 第36课
[00:02.12]A chance in a million | 百万分之一的机遇
[00:09.59]What was the chance in a million? | 那百万分之一的机遇是什么？
[00:13.45]We are less credulous than we used to be. | 我们不再像以往那样轻信别人了。
[00:17.13]In the nineteenth century, a novelist would bring his story to a conclusion | 在19世纪，小说家常在小说结尾处给读者安排一系列巧合
[00:22.60]by presenting his readers with a series of coincidences--most of them wildly improbable. | ——大部分都是牵强附会，极不可能发生的。
[00:30.29]Readers happily accepted the fact that an obscure maidservant was really the hero's mother. | 读者们乐于接受这样的情节：一个不知名的女仆原来是主人公的母亲。
[00:37.78]A long-lost brother, who was presumed dead, was really alive all the time | 一位失散多年、被认为已死去的兄弟其实一直活着，
[00:44.07]and wickedly plotting to bring about the hero's downfall. And so on. | 并且一直在阴险地策划毁灭主人公。等等。
[00:50.02]Modern readers would find such naive solutions totally unacceptable. | 现代读者会觉得这种天真的解决方案完全无法接受。
[00:56.13]Yet, in real life, circumstances do sometimes conspire to bring about coincidences which anyone but a nineteenth century novelist would find incredible. | 然而在现实生活中，有时确实会出现各种情况巧合在一起，这种巧合除了19世纪的小说家谁都会觉得难以置信。
[01:09.39]When I was a boy, my grandfather told me how a German taxi driver, Franz Bussman, | 我小时候，祖父给我讲过一位德国出租车司机弗朗兹·巴斯曼的故事，
[01:16.37]found a brother who was thought to have been killed twenty years before. | 他找到了一位被认为20年前已经死去的兄弟。
[01:21.39]While on a walking tour with his wife, he stopped to talk to a workman. | 一次他同妻子徒步旅行途中，停下来与一位工人交谈。
[01:27.10]After they had gone on, Mrs. Bussman commented on the workman's close resemblance to her husband | 他们继续往前走后，巴斯曼夫人评论说那工人长得酷似她丈夫，
[01:34.34]and even suggested that he might be his brother. | 甚至暗示他可能就是丈夫的兄弟。
[01:38.71]Franz poured scorn on the idea, | 弗朗兹对此嗤之以鼻，
[01:42.09]pointing out that his brother had been killed in action during the war. | 指出他的兄弟已在战争中阵亡。
[01:47.35]Though Mrs. Bussman was fully acquainted with this story, | 虽然巴斯曼夫人完全了解这个故事，
[01:51.70]she thought that there was a chance in a million that she might be right. | 她仍认为自己有百万分之一的可能性猜对。
[01:57.09]A few days later, she sent a boy to the workman to ask him if his name was Hans Bussman. | 几天之后，她派了一个男孩去问那工人是否名叫汉斯·巴斯曼。
[02:05.01]Needless to say, the man's name was Hans Bussman and he really was Franz's long-lost brother. | 不出所料，那人果然名叫汉斯·巴斯曼，真是弗朗兹失散多年的兄弟。
[02:13.94]When the brothers were reunited, | 兄弟团聚之时，
[02:16.20]Hans explained how it was that he was still alive. | 汉斯解释了自己是怎么活下来的。
[02:20.36]After having been wounded towards the end of the war, | 战争即将结束时他负了伤，
[02:24.73]he had been sent to hospital and was separated from his unit. | 被送进医院，与部队失去了联系。
[02:29.84]The hospital had been bombed and Hans had made his way back into Western Germany on foot. | 医院遭到轰炸，汉斯步行回到了西德。
[02:37.64]Meanwhile, his unit was lost and all records of him had been destroyed. | 与此同时，他的部队失踪了，关于他的所有记录都被毁掉了。
[02:43.74]Hans returned to his family home, but the house had been bombed | 汉斯回到了老家，但房子已被炸毁，
[02:48.37]and no one in the neighbourhood knew what had become of the inhabitants. | 邻居中谁也不知道当年的住户的下落。
[02:53.03]Assuming that his family had been killed during an air raid, | 汉斯以为家人都在空袭中遇难了，
[02:57.00]Hans settled down in a village fifty miles away where he had remained ever since. | 便在50英里外的一个村庄定居下来，此后一直住在那里。
